@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Complex DMN Decision Requirements Diagram for loan
'                 underwriting: 13 decisions in a deep dependency tree, multiple
'                 input data sources, two reused Business Knowledge Models, and
'                 three Knowledge Sources providing invocation authority.
'   usecase:      Teaching large DRDs: deep decision trees, fan-out/fan-in,
'                 reused knowledge models, authority edges, and how to attach an
'                 illustrative decision-table note showing one decision's logic.
'   complexity:   complex
'   domain:       finance / underwriting
' ============================================================================

title DMN Decision Requirements - Complex Underwriting DRD

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Large DRDs are read TOP-UP or BOTTOM-UP:
'   - Top-down:  start at the final decision ("Offer") and ask "what do I need
'                to know to produce this?" — each answer is an upstream dep.
'   - Bottom-up: start from raw inputs and decisions at the leaves and compose.
'
' Patterns visible here:
'   1. Fan-out:  one input/decision feeds many downstream decisions.
'   2. Fan-in:   a decision aggregates several upstream decisions.
'   3. Shared BK: a Business Knowledge Model invoked by many decisions
'                 (drawn once, dashed to each consumer).
'   4. Authority: Knowledge Sources (regulations, policies) govern decisions
'                 via dashed knowledge-requirement edges.
'   5. DAG:       the graph is acyclic — no decision (transitively) needs itself.
'
' A DRD shows ONLY topology (who needs whom). The LOGIC of any single decision
' lives in its "boxed expression" — most often a decision table. The note on
' "Risk Class" below illustrates what that payload looks like.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' === Input Data ===
usecase "Application\nForm"     as AppForm
usecase "Credit\nBureau Pull"   as CreditBureau
usecase "Collateral\nAppraisal" as Appraisal
usecase "Income\nDocs"          as IncomeDocs
usecase "Market\nData"          as MarketData

' === Business Knowledge Models (reusable logic) ===
rectangle "Risk Grade\nModel"  as RiskGradeModel <<businessKnowledge>>
rectangle "Policy\nRules"      as PolicyRules     <<businessKnowledge>>

' === Knowledge Sources (authorities) ===
file "Regulation\nZ"           as RegZ
file "Underwriting\nManual"    as UWManual
file "Capital\nPolicy"         as CapitalPolicy

' === Decisions (13) — dependency tree ===
' Tier 1 — intake / verification
rectangle "Identity\nVerified"   as Identity   <<decision>>
rectangle "Income\nVerified"     as Income     <<decision>>
rectangle "Collateral\nValue"    as Collateral <<decision>>

' Tier 2 — scoring
rectangle "Credit\nScore"        as CreditScore <<decision>>
rectangle "DTI\nRatio"           as DTI         <<decision>>
rectangle "LTV\nRatio"           as LTV         <<decision>>

' Tier 3 — classification
rectangle "Risk\nClass"          as RiskClass   <<decision>>
rectangle "Policy\nFlags"        as PolicyFlags <<decision>>

' Tier 4 — terms
rectangle "Eligibility"          as Eligibility <<decision>>
rectangle "Rate\nTier"           as RateTier    <<decision>>
rectangle "Loan\nSize"           as LoanSize    <<decision>>

' Tier 5 — final
rectangle "Offer"                as Offer       <<decision>>

' === Information requirements (solid) ===
' Inputs -> Tier 1
AppForm     --> Identity
IncomeDocs  --> Income
Appraisal   --> Collateral
CreditBureau --> CreditScore
MarketData  --> Collateral

' Tier 1 -> Tier 2
Income      --> DTI
Collateral  --> LTV

' Tier 2 -> Tier 3
CreditScore --> RiskClass
DTI         --> RiskClass
LTV         --> RiskClass
RiskClass   --> PolicyFlags

' Tier 3 -> Tier 4
RiskClass   --> Eligibility
PolicyFlags --> Eligibility
RiskClass   --> RateTier
RiskClass   --> LoanSize
Collateral  --> LoanSize

' Tier 4 -> Tier 5 (fan-in)
Eligibility --> Offer
RateTier    --> Offer
LoanSize    --> Offer

' === Knowledge requirements (dashed) ===
' Risk Grade Model reused across scoring decisions.
CreditScore ..> RiskGradeModel : <<authority>>
RiskClass   ..> RiskGradeModel : <<authority>>

' Policy Rules reused across compliance + sizing.
PolicyFlags ..> PolicyRules : <<authority>>
Eligibility ..> PolicyRules : <<authority>>
LoanSize    ..> PolicyRules : <<authority>>

' Knowledge Sources govern decisions.
Offer       ..> RegZ          : <<authority>>
Eligibility ..> UWManual      : <<authority>>
LoanSize    ..> CapitalPolicy : <<authority>>

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ILLUSTRATIVE DECISION TABLE — the "payload" of the Risk Class decision.
' The DRD above says Risk Class exists and what feeds it. This note shows WHAT
' ITS LOGIC MIGHT LOOK LIKE as a decision table (hit policy + input/output cols).
' It is illustrative only — the table is NOT part of the DRD graph itself.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note right of RiskClass
  **Illustrative decision table for "Risk Class"**
  (boxed expression — the decision's payload, not part of the DRD topology)
  --
  Hit Policy: **UNIQUE** (at most one matching rule)
  --
  | Inputs               |             | Output       |
  | Credit Score | DTI   | LTV         | Risk Class   |
  |------------|--------|-------------|--------------|
  | >= 750     | < 36%  | <= 80%      | A (prime)    |
  | 680-749    | < 43%  | <= 90%      | B (near)     |
  | 620-679    | < 50%  | <= 95%      | C (subprime) |
  | < 620      | --     | --          | D (decline)  |
  --
  Hit policies: U=unique, F=first, P=priority, C=collect, A=any, O=output order
end note

note bottom of Offer
  **Reading a deep DRD**
  Offer (final) depends on 3 decisions, each of which depends on more.
  Trace a SOLID edge backward to find every input that ultimately influences
  the offer. Trace a DASHED edge to find every rule/regulation that constrains
  the logic along the way.
end note
@enduml
